{
  "feature_instruction": "## Design Tokens\n- Navbar: bg-[#1A3A6D] text-white\n- Sidebar: bg-[#1A3A6D] text-white\n- Primary CTA: bg-[#E87A00] hover:bg-[#D46C00] text-white font-semibold rounded-md px-6 py-2 transition-all duration-200\n- Secondary CTA: bg-gray-200 hover:bg-gray-300 text-[#212121] font-semibold rounded-md px-6 py-2 transition-all duration-200\n- Brand text accent: text-[#E87A00]\n- Section bg: bg-[#F5F5F5]\n- Card: bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-4\n- Section container: <section className=\"py-8 px-4\"><div className=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto\"> (for admin pages)\n- Body: text-[#212121] leading-relaxed\n- Table Header: bg-gray-100 text-gray-600\n- Input/Select: border border-gray-300 rounded-md p-2 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-[#E87A00] focus:border-transparent\n\nThe Admin Portal Frontend provides a comprehensive interface for managing Prakash Stores' products, categories, brands, orders, and events. It is structured around a shared `AdminLayout` component which provides a sidebar navigation and ensures all admin pages are protected by authentication, requiring an 'ADMIN' role. Each administrative section (Products, Categories, Brands, Orders, Events) has its own dedicated page, which composes a table component for listing items and a form component (often in a dialog) for creating or editing items. All data interactions are handled by generated service functions that call the respective backend `/api/v1/admin` endpoints.\n\n### AdminLayout.tsx\nThis component serves as the main shell for all admin pages. It utilizes `useAuth()` to check if the current user is authenticated and has the 'ADMIN' role. If not, it redirects to the login page. It renders a sidebar with navigation links to `AdminDashboardPage`, `AdminProductsPage`, `AdminCategoriesPage`, `AdminBrandsPage`, `AdminOrdersPage`, and `AdminEventsPage`. The main content area renders the child routes. The sidebar links should use the brand's deep blue for background and white for text, with saffron orange for active link indicators.\n\n### AdminDashboardPage.tsx\nThis is the landing page for the admin portal. It should display a welcoming message like \"Welcome, Admin!\" and potentially summary statistics or quick links to other admin sections. For now, it will be a simple page with a heading and a brief description.\n\n### AdminProductsPage.tsx\nThis page is responsible for managing products. It orchestrates the `ProductTable` component to display a list of products and provides functionality to add new products or edit existing ones using the `ProductForm` component. It will fetch all products using the generated `productService.getAllProducts()` function. When a product is selected for editing, the `ProductForm` will be pre-filled with the product's data fetched via `productService.getProductById(productId)`. New products are created via `productService.createProduct(CreateProductRequest)`, and updates via `productService.updateProduct(productId, UpdateProductRequest)`. Deletion is handled by `productService.deleteProduct(productId)` via `DeleteProductDialog`.\n\n### ProductTable.tsx\nThis component displays a paginated table of products. Each row should include product details such as `name`, `price` (formatted in ₹), `category.name`, `brand.name`, and `active` status. It provides action buttons for 'Edit' and 'Delete'. The 'Edit' button should trigger the `ProductForm` with the selected product's data. The 'Delete' button should open the `DeleteProductDialog`.\n\n### ProductForm.tsx\nThis component is a form for creating or editing a product. It includes fields for `name`, `description`, `price`, `imageUrl`, `categoryId`, `brandId`, and `active` status. It should fetch available categories and brands using `productService.getAllCategories()` and `productService.getAllBrands()` to populate dropdowns. Upon submission, it calls either `productService.createProduct` or `productService.updateProduct` based on whether a product ID is provided. The `imageUrl` field should allow for direct URL input or integrate with a future media gallery foundation feature if available.\n\n### DeleteProductDialog.tsx\nThis component is a confirmation dialog for deleting a product. It takes a product ID and a callback function for deletion. Upon confirmation, it calls `productService.deleteProduct(productId)` and then triggers the callback to refresh the product list.\n\n### AdminCategoriesPage.tsx\nThis page manages product categories. It uses `CategoryTable` to list categories and `CategoryForm` for creation and editing. It fetches categories using `productService.getAllCategories()`. Creation is handled by `productService.createCategory(CategoryDto)`, updates by `productService.updateCategory(categoryId, CategoryDto)`, and deletion by `productService.deleteCategory(categoryId)`.\n\n### CategoryTable.tsx\nThis component displays a table of product categories, showing `id`, `name`, and `description`. It includes 'Edit' and 'Delete' actions, similar to `ProductTable`.\n\n### CategoryForm.tsx\nThis form is for creating or editing a product category. It has fields for `name` and `description`. It calls `productService.createCategory` or `productService.updateCategory` on submission.\n\n### AdminBrandsPage.tsx\nThis page manages product brands. It uses `BrandTable` to list brands and `BrandForm` for creation and editing. It fetches brands using `productService.getAllBrands()`. Creation is handled by `productService.createBrand(BrandDto)`, updates by `productService.updateBrand(brandId, BrandDto)`, and deletion by `productService.deleteBrand(brandId)`.\n\n### BrandTable.tsx\nThis component displays a table of product brands, showing `id`, `name`, and `description`. It includes 'Edit' and 'Delete' actions.\n\n### BrandForm.tsx\nThis form is for creating or editing a product brand. It has fields for `name` and `description`. It calls `productService.createBrand` or `productService.updateBrand` on submission.\n\n### AdminOrdersPage.tsx\nThis page manages customer orders. It uses `OrderTable` to display a list of orders and `OrderDetailView` to show detailed information about a selected order. It fetches all orders using the generated `orderService.getAllOrders()` function. It also allows updating the order status via `orderService.updateOrderStatus(orderId, newStatus)`.\n\n### OrderTable.tsx\nThis component displays a table of customer orders. Each row should include `orderId`, `orderDate`, `totalAmount` (formatted in ₹), and `orderStatus`. It should provide an action to 'View Details' which opens the `OrderDetailView` for the selected order. It also includes a dropdown or button to update the `orderStatus`.\n\n### OrderDetailView.tsx\nThis component displays a detailed view of a single order, typically in a dialog or drawer. It fetches order details using `orderService.getOrderById(orderId)`. It shows `orderId`, `orderDate`, `totalAmount` (formatted in ₹), `orderStatus`, `shippingAddress`, `city`, `state`, `pincode`, `shippingMethod`, `paymentId`, `paymentStatus`, and a list of `orderItems` including `productName`, `quantity`, and `unitPrice` (formatted in ₹).\n\n### AdminEventsPage.tsx\nThis page manages in-store events. It uses `EventTable` to list events and `EventForm` for creation and editing. It fetches events using the generated `eventService.getAllEvents()` function. Creation is handled by `eventService.createEvent(CreateEventRequest)`, updates by `eventService.updateEvent(id, CreateEventRequest)`, and deletion by `eventService.deleteEvent(id)`.\n\n### EventTable.tsx\nThis component displays a table of events, showing `id`, `name`, `eventDate`, `location`, and `imageUrl`. It includes 'Edit' and 'Delete' actions.\n\n### EventForm.tsx\nThis form is for creating or editing an event. It has fields for `name`, `description`, `eventDate`, `location`, and `imageUrl`. It calls `eventService.createEvent` or `eventService.updateEvent` on submission. The `eventDate` should use a date/time picker.\n",
